Aerial

Two ways the oil and gas industry puts carbon into the air — gas it burns and
gas it leaks — on one map, under one date window.

  make serve     # static server on :8000
  make test      # the reducer's rules, in node
  make audit     # the page against the dd brand, headless
  make vendor    # re-vendor maplibre, duckdb, the dd design system and inter

Four detection layers, all drawn at once, one quarter grid over all of them:

  - S2 and S3: Sentinel-2 hotspots by day and Sentinel-3 hotspots by night,
    read live from burnoff's service (github.com/data-desk-eco/burnoff) for
    the view.
  - VNF: VIIRS Nightfire, from the eog tables in the Data Desk archive, read
    through DuckDB-Wasm, which sees flares at night and measures their
    radiant heat.
  - Methane: dated plume observations from Carbon Mapper, IMEO, SRON,
    Google and Data Desk. Most carry a measured release rate; Google
    carries a modeled methane enhancement (ppm·m) and a model confidence, shown
    in place of a rate so it is never read as a mass.

Aerial reads and draws. It publishes nothing: the archive it draws is built by
the etl repo, and the hotspots by burnoff.

Shape says whether a feature burned or leaked, and colour says only how much,
each family on its own scale of the Data Desk intensity ramp. The scales are
not comparable, so the key states each one's units and its rows are the
colour bands: turn a band off to filter it out, turn them all off to take the
layer off the map.

A link with ?embed before its # draws the map alone under one selection:
no panels, the selection's caption at the top left, the imagery lifted, a
plume's whole outline centred in the view, no candidate sources (an
attributed one stays), and a click anywhere opens the
whole app on the same link. ?embed&logo adds the Data Desk mark.

  https://aerial.datadesk.eco/?embed#map=14.69/50.004/18.621&plume=<id>

With no selection it draws a region, and ?only=plumes (or vnf, s2, s3)
reads and draws that family alone. A region of plumes is captioned with
the start of its window and the providers it holds.

  https://aerial.datadesk.eco/?embed&only=plumes#map=3.3/53/14

It is for a figure in an article and for stills: a headless browser
photographs it once `aerial.selection()` is set (a region has none),
`aerial.map.loaded()` is true and #status is empty (coal-scroll
src/shot.js). For a still whose plume grows, `aerial.mask.grid()` gives
the plume image's top left and the size of one of its pixels in the view
([x, y, w, h] in px, nothing for a bare outline), and
`await aerial.mask.show(false)` takes the plume's mask off the map. For
a still of a region whose points appear one by one, `aerial.points.at()`
gives where each drawn point sits in the view ([x, y] in px, oldest
first) and `await aerial.points.show(false)` takes the points off.

?layer=<provider>/<table> draws a table of the archive on request, more
than one separated by commas. A private table (data-desk-raw tables/,
which its own index.json lists) is read through /private/, behind the
Data Desk login: a page that names one goes through the login first, and
nobody else can read it. A detections table joins the plumes; any other
table draws its geometry, named on hover. The map opens on the layers
when the link names no view.

  https://aerial.datadesk.eco/?layer=ghgsat/detections,data-desk/drainage
  https://aerial.datadesk.eco/?layer=data-desk/fields

/private/ needs its read key once, from Secret Manager to the worker:
gcloud auth login, then make private-key.

Where things live:

  web/config.js      the whole app declaration
  web/about.js       the intro and its sources
  web/heat.js        the far surface, below zoom 5
  web/layers.js      marking, ramp and colour policy for every layer
  web/key.js         the key: a group of colour bands per family
  web/sites.js       facility outlines, and what a selection lights
  web/layer.js       ?layer=: archive tables on request, the private ones too
  web/shell/         the map shell: mount, panels, key, quarters, card, drawer
  web/card/          one card header, one body per feature kind
  web/flaring/       the live S2 and S3 reads, the VNF reader, the scales
  web/methane/       plume reader, attribution, candidates, overlay, mask
  web/vendor/        the dd design system, DuckDB, MapLibre, quack, Inter

CLAUDE.md carries the invariants: read them before changing how a rate is
computed.

Zero npm dependencies. Every library is vendored; everything else is browser
built-ins.

References

Elvidge et al. (2013) VIIRS Nightfire: satellite pyrometry at night
https://doi.org/10.3390/rs5094423
Caseiro et al. (2018) Persistent hot spot detection and characterisation using
SLSTR
https://doi.org/10.3390/rs10071118
Jacob et al. (2022) Quantifying methane emissions from the global scale down to
point sources
https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-22-9617-2022
